--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.1 Trinity — ACM MMSys 2026
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

TOPIC: Cloud VR gaming QoE via action-type-aware latency exploitation.

CORE INSIGHT:
In an FPS VR game, users do NOT have uniform latency tolerance across different
action types. There are at least three distinct interactions:
  - Head rotation (looking/aiming): most latency-sensitive
  - Hand/gun motion (aiming fine adjustments): moderately sensitive
  - Background/body updates: least sensitive

We validated this through a user study with ~30 participants using an
open-source FPS VR game.

SYSTEM DESIGN:
  - Head motion: send a "super panorama" frame to the client and do late
    warping on-device, decoupling viewport updates from network round-trip.
  - Hand/gun: rendered on the headset with an intentionally injected delay
    that keeps it temporally coherent with server-rendered background.
  - Background: rendered server-side, accepts the full network round-trip.

The injected delay for the gun is the key trick — it bridges the latency gap
between head motion (client-side, ~instant) and background (server-side, ~slow),
preventing the perceptual mismatch that breaks immersion.

RESULT: Comparable QoE to local streaming while reducing bandwidth by 60% and
GPU utilization by 40%.

KEY TAKEAWAY:
Human behavioral measurement in an interactive context (FPS game) directly
drives system design decisions. The "measure humans first, optimize second"
approach opened up a design space that pure systems thinking would miss.


