### 2.2 MARS — ACM Multimedia 2026
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

TOPIC: Motion-aware adaptive bitrate for real-time VR streaming.

CORE QUESTION:
Given that users' dynamic visual acuity decreases during fast head rotation
(a well-established perceptual phenomenon), can we systematically exploit this
to reduce streaming bandwidth in real-time VR without perceptible quality loss?

TWO CORE CHALLENGES:
  1. Synchronization: bitrate adaptation must be precisely timed with head motion
     phases, accounting for MTP latency (70–80ms end-to-end).
  2. Magnitude: how aggressively to reduce quality — too much breaks QoE.

SOLUTIONS:
  - Kinematics-based state machine: tracks head acceleration/deceleration to
    identify motion phases (Slow / Speed-up / Fast / Decelerate) and proactively
    triggers bitrate changes ahead of the MTP delay.
  - Perceptually-guided rate adaptation: uses the JND (Just Noticeable
    Difference) principle — maps head rotation speed to the minimum bitrate
    that preserves perceived quality (measured via PSPNR metric).

EVALUATION:
  - 31-participant user study with bias-controlled design: fraction of users
    perceiving a difference between MARS and Fixed barely increases from 47%
    (Fixed vs Fixed baseline) to 53%, confirming perceptual transparency.
  - Emulation benchmark: 150 real network traces (4G/WiFi/5G).
  - Result: 10–19% bandwidth savings, zero perceptible quality degradation.

MARS is plug-and-play — integrates into ALVR with no game engine modification.

KEY TAKEAWAY:
A human perceptual model (JND + motion masking) directly translates into an
engineering decision (bitrate reduction schedule). The challenge is not the
model itself but the real-time synchronization problem under system latency.


